messages:
maria: Hey — running into a weird issue when using the JS SDK inside Cloudflare Workers (ESM build). Auth header is being stripped on some requests and retries fail with 401s. Anyone seen this?

maria: Minimal repro: import the SDK as ESM, call client.chat.completions.create(...) from a worker. First request sometimes goes through, retry from our retry wrapper gets 401. No Node APIs available so we can't use node-fetch.

maria: Relevant snippet:
```js
// worker entry (ESM)
import Redwood from 'redwood-inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: ENV.RW_KEY });
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(handler(event.request));
});
async function handler(req) {
  return client.chat.completions.create({ model: 'gpt-test', messages: [{role:'user', content:'hi'}] });
}
```

jon: Hmm. Two things to check: 1) are you accidentally mutating the Request/Headers object in the retry path? CF Workers reuses header objects in some libs. 2) Is your SDK using `request.clone()` or manually copying headers?

maria: I'm not cloning. We use our retry wrapper that replays the original request by reusing the generated fetch options. Maybe that's the problem.

sam: Another common gotcha: some auth shims add `Authorization` to a custom header bag, then when building a new Request they rely on Node's `headers` iteration order. In Workers you need to use the Headers API and create a fresh Headers() with `for (const [k,v] of original.headers)` copying over.

lee: Also check for automatic newline or unicode normalization in your token string — CF will happily pass it but if you slice/format before retry it can change.

maria: Good tips. The SDK currently does this in the runtime layer:
```js
// simplified inside SDK runtime
const opts = { method: 'POST', headers: baseHeaders, body };
return fetch(endpoint, opts);
```
If baseHeaders is the same object reused across retries that might be the cause.

jon: Exactly. We should make the SDK do a defensive clone: `const headers = new Headers(baseHeaders);` then set auth on that. That is compatible with Workers and Node.

sam: TypeScript side: I ran into a typing quirk when switching to the ESM entrypoint for workers — some ambient node types leak and `HeadersInit` resolves weirdly. For the SDK build, publishing a separate `edge` export with `types: "dist/edge/index.d.ts"` and an edge-targeted declaration might help.

maria: Would a small runtime-only shim fix the immediate issue? Something like `edge-shim.ts`:
```ts
export function buildFetchOpts(baseHeaders, token) {
  const headers = new Headers(baseHeaders);
  headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
  return { headers, method: 'POST' };
}
```
Then SDK uses `buildFetchOpts` in edge builds.

lee: That's reasonable. We could ship a tiny `edge-shims/` folder in the package and make the package.json exports map `./runtime.js` -> `./dist/node/runtime.js`, `./runtime.edge.js` -> `./dist/edge/runtime.js` (ESM) so bundlers pick the right file.

sam: Agree. Also call out in the README that workers users should use the `edge` entry to avoid `node:` polyfills. Many folks import the default entry and get Node-only code pulled in which breaks on CF.

maria: One more wrinkle: our retry middleware currently serializes the Request options as JSON then replays. That loses `Headers` prototype methods and sometimes the token ends up as `Authorization: undefined` if we used header getters. We should serialize to a plain array of header entries and rehydrate with `new Headers(entries)` on replay.

jon: +1. Implementation plan:
- change retry logic to store header entries not a headers object
- defensive clone headers before mutating
- release `dist/edge` build without Node polyfills and update package.json exports
- add a small TypeScript declaration for edge builds to avoid ambient node types

lee: I can pick up the package export and build changes. Will wire CI to build both `dist/node` and `dist/edge`.

sam: I can do the TS declarations and add a test that runs in Miniflare CI to catch header mutation regressions.

maria: Awesome. For the short term, does anyone have a quick workaround I can drop into the worker?

jon: Quick hack: copy headers before each fetch and ensure auth is explicitly set.
```js
const headers = new Headers(req.headers);
headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${ENV.RW_KEY}`);
await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', headers });
```
That should avoid the 401s until the SDK patch lands.

maria: Thanks! I'll try that and open a GH issue with a failing test.

devops-bot: CI pipeline started for branch fix/edge-shim (build 482) :rocket:

lee: :eyes: watching the PR.

sam: :shipit: once tests pass.

maria: Filing issue and starting a draft PR now. Will ping y'all when it's up. Thanks everyone.
